      {
        "kind": "markdown",
        "text": "## Reading the little theorem\n\nIf $p$ is prime, every integer $a$ satisfies $a^p\\equiv a\\pmod p$. The prime $p$ specifies the modulus, and $a$ may be any integer. For example, $2^5=32\\equiv2\\pmod5$, which Lean confirms:",
